  control-l2-resident  0.069 to 0.875       0.058 to 0.879   n=2:0.728  n=4:0.028  n=8:0.122

  W is the measured time of ONE full pass over every expert's weight block, read off
  the n=1 rung. Both alphas are a fitted slope over that intercept, so every unit of
  time cancels and no bandwidth, byte count or ridge enters either number.

  POOLED (median over the non-control models): alpha is in 0.535 to 0.969, 90% interval 0.504 to 0.970
  against the refit's 0.558 (0.529-0.588) pooled and 0.570 at GROUP_SIZE_M=1.

## P2, reported and deliberately NOT gated

  A step in four points is a pattern, not a test. This study has already retracted one
  monotone-in-expert-count reading that was an artefact of pooling, so the ordering is
  printed and left for a design that varies the footprint continuously.

  deepseek-v2-lite         11.0 MiB/expert  below L2   alpha 0.082 to 0.885
  qwen2-57b-a14b           35.0 MiB/expert  below L2   alpha 0.443 to 0.965
  deepseek-v3              56.0 MiB/expert  below L2   alpha 0.627 to 0.981
  mixtral-8x7b            224.0 MiB/expert  above L2   alpha 0.640 to 0.973

## what else differs between aliased and normal

  BOUNDED. L2 capacity. The aliased variant leaves L2 to the activations, whose stream
  is 4.55% of the weight stream at the worst rung, so the most the freed
  capacity can be worth is that fraction of D. The output write is identical in both.

  BOUNDED BY MEASUREMENT. TLB, page behaviour and code path. The L2-resident control
  ran the same ladder on a geometry whose PER-EXPERT block fits in L2, so NORMAL
  has no HBM re-read to save. Its W is 0.0308 ms and its alpha brackets 0.069 to 0.875.
  Whatever survives there is not weight traffic.

  BOUNDED BY THE BRACKET. L2 service, and the cache-set distribution that makes it
  worse. Both variants push n W bytes through L2, and the aliased variant pins every
  one of them to a single BLOCK_K x BLOCK_N tile, so its loads land on a handful of L2
  slices and are served more slowly than the aggregate L2 bandwidth would suggest.
  Whatever that costs shows up in the ALIASED ladder's own slope, which is r above,
  and the two estimators bracket the truth for any composition of L2 and HBM service
  between a sum and a max. Narrowing it means spreading the aliased tile across more
  slices, which this design does not do because a spread alias has no closed form to
  check the output against.

  ABSORBED BY CONSTRUCTION. Any cost that scales with the extra-tile count and is not a
  weight re-read lands in alpha, because (n-1) is the regressor. That is a property of
  the estimator and no control can remove it.

## gates

  [PASS] ISA: the aliased kernel issued the same global loads
          20 rungs compared. First, mixtral-8x7b|t1|bm16: normal 6 global-load instructions (ld.global 6, cp.async 0); aliased 6 (ld.global 6, cp.async 0). Every rung's two launches are ONE compiled kernel. The constexpr-aliased kernel, the naive way to write this, issues 5 and DID fold, which is the hazard happening in front of you
  [PASS] correctness: each variant reproduced its closed form
          40 checks at relative RMS <= 1e-03; worst 1.54e-07
  [PASS] placebo: two identical launches differ by far less than D
          worst rung qwen2-57b-a14b|t4|bm16: two identical configurations differ by 0.0042 ms against a D of 0.1262 ms, 3.3% (limit 10%)
  [FAIL] signal: the weight read is most of what the kernel does
          weakest one-tile rung deepseek-v3|t1|bm16 has D(1) at 5.3% of its own time (limit 25%). Below that the difference is measuring something the weight-read label does not cover
  [FAIL] form: D(n) is affine in (n-1), as W(1+alpha(n-1)) requires
          worst R^2 0.4179 on deepseek-v2-lite (limit 0.97). Below it the functional form is wrong and slope-over-intercept is not alpha
  [PASS] control: an L2-resident expert shows no extra-tile cost
          the control's bracket is 0.069 to 0.875 on a W of 0.0308 ms, against a top end of 0.885 for the weakest real model. It must be consistent with zero to within 0.15: its re-reads hit L2, so anything it does show is the size of an extra-tile cost that is not weight traffic
  [NOT TESTABLE] resolution: the interval can separate the three candidates
          90% interval is 0.466 wide (limit 0.11). The candidates span 0.1 to 0.558, and an interval wider than half the largest gap between adjacent candidates cannot pick one. r, the aliased ladder's per-tile cost over one weight read, is 6.971 to 19.070, and r is what sets the bracket's width: the two estimators differ by roughly 2r/(1-r^2). Narrowing this needs a cheaper aliased ladder, which means spreading the aliased tile across more L2 slices rather than pinning every load to one
  [PASS] P1: the ablation agrees with the refit, alpha = 0.558
          measured alpha is in 0.535 to 0.969 before noise and 0.504 to 0.970 after it, against the refit's 0.529-0.588: they overlap. the interval 0.504-0.970 contains today's refit

VERDICT: REFUTED or VOID. 2 gate(s) failed: signal: the weight read is most of what the kernel does; form: D(n) is affine in (n-1), as W(1+alpha(n-1)) requires

